      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure this is correct, on two accounts :\n• In the old world, this member is guaranteed to be non-null because nai.satisfies(req). The reasoning is that if there is at least one NAI that satisfies this request, then it\u0027s impossible that this request is not satisfied. Also, this code is only called after a rematch, not before (otherwise it would be possible that a NAI could satisfy this request but not be assigned yet, and mSatisfier would be null).\n → But I\u0027m not sure how true that holds in the new world. It is certainly possible that an NAI can satisfy a *request* and that request doesn\u0027t have a satisfier. But mSatisfier is not the satisfier for the request, it\u0027s the satisfier for the NRI, and I do think that if any of the requests has at least one NAI that can satisfy it then we have the same guarantee as before. I think.\n• mSatisfier here is the satisfier for the NRI, and at this point we don\u0027t know what request it satisfies, so it doesn\u0027t necessarily make sense to compare its score to the score of the candidate.\n\nReally what this function should do is probably to \n① figure out which of the requests mSatisfier satisfies\n\n  NetworkRequest satisfiedRequest \u003d null;\n  for (NetworkRequest req : nri.mRequests) {\n    if (req.isRequest() \u0026\u0026 mSatisfier.satisfies(req)) {\n      satisfiedRequest \u003d req;\n      break;\n    } // NOTE\n  }\n\n② figure out if this candidate can possibly beat the current satisfier\n  \n  return nai.satisfies(req) \u0026\u0026 mSatisfier.getCurrentScore() \u003c nai.getCurrentScoreAsValidated();\n\n...I think that should work.\nObserve that in NOTE we can add code like the following :\n\n - } // NOTE\n + } else if (nai.satisfies(req)) {\n +   return true;\n + }\n\n...because if that\u0027s true, then this candidate can satisfy a higher-priority request than the current satisfier. But in practice, and for the same reason as above, it\u0027s never possible to enter this test because if this candidate could satisfy a higher-prio request than the current satisfier, well this candidate would have won the rematch and would be the current satisfier. This probably warrants a comment.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3554,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 3554,
        "endChar": 45
      },
